1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,893
Welcome to Day 2 of SQL Mastery!

2
00:00:02,893 --> 00:00:07,082
Today we're solving a real-world data quality challenge.

3
00:00:07,532 --> 00:00:13,337
In this episode: Understand the business problem; Learn data quality validation; Master

4
00:00:13,337 --> 00:00:17,540
reconciliation techniques; Build trustworthy financial reports.

5
00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:20,958
Part one.

6
00:00:20,958 --> 00:00:22,919
The Business Challenge.

7
00:00:25,569 --> 00:00:29,702
An e-commerce company is facing serious data issues

8
00:00:30,152 --> 00:00:34,366
Finance has discovered multiple problems in our payment data

9
00:00:34,816 --> 00:00:36,626
That sounds concerning!

10
00:00:36,626 --> 00:00:38,357
What kind of problems?

11
00:00:41,007 --> 00:00:44,014
Duplicate payments appearing in the system

12
00:00:44,464 --> 00:00:47,076
Successful orders missing their payments

13
00:00:47,526 --> 00:00:50,278
Payment amounts don't match order totals

14
00:00:50,728 --> 00:00:53,538
Payments referencing non-existent orders

15
00:00:53,988 --> 00:00:57,505
Invalid records entering financial reports

16
00:00:57,955 --> 00:01:01,160
Daily books cannot be closed with confidence

17
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:07,211
These issues have serious business consequences!

18
00:01:09,861 --> 00:01:10,658
Part two.

19
00:01:10,658 --> 00:01:12,694
Understanding the Data.

20
00:01:15,344 --> 00:01:18,386
We have two main data sources to work with

21
00:01:18,836 --> 00:01:22,528
First, the orders table from our sales platform

22
00:01:22,978 --> 00:01:26,171
And the payments table from the payment processor?

23
00:01:28,821 --> 00:01:32,199
The orders table tracks every customer purchase

24
00:01:34,849 --> 00:01:38,054
The payments table tracks all payment attempts

25
00:01:40,704 --> 00:01:43,850
Data flows from two independent systems

26
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:47,546
Part three.

27
00:01:47,546 --> 00:01:49,449
Examining Real Data.

28
00:01:52,099 --> 00:01:54,839
Can you spot the data quality issues here?

29
00:01:57,489 --> 00:02:00,252
Let me highlight the problems in the orders!

30
00:02:02,902 --> 00:02:06,373
Order 2004 has a missing customer I D

31
00:02:06,823 --> 00:02:10,864
Order 2005 has a negative quantity of minus 2

32
00:02:11,314 --> 00:02:14,506
Order 2006 has a missing unit price

33
00:02:14,956 --> 00:02:18,521
Order 2003 is cancelled but has a payment

34
00:02:21,171 --> 00:02:24,038
The payments table has even more issues!

35
00:02:26,688 --> 00:02:30,264
Payment 501 appears twice as duplicates

36
00:02:30,714 --> 00:02:36,217
Payment 502 amount is 100 but order expects 120

37
00:02:36,667 --> 00:02:39,872
Payment 503 is for a cancelled order

38
00:02:40,322 --> 00:02:45,918
Payment 504 references non-existent order 9999

39
00:02:46,368 --> 00:02:50,594
Payment 505 has both failed and successful versions

40
00:02:53,244 --> 00:02:54,075
Part four.

41
00:02:54,075 --> 00:02:55,902
Building the Solution.

42
00:02:58,552 --> 00:03:02,326
We'll solve this with a structured six step approach

43
00:03:02,776 --> 00:03:06,653
Each step validates, cleans, or reconciles the data

44
00:03:07,103 --> 00:03:10,099
And we never silently discard bad records!

45
00:03:12,749 --> 00:03:16,023
Validate all orders against quality rules

46
00:03:16,473 --> 00:03:19,387
Store invalid orders with rejection reasons

47
00:03:19,837 --> 00:03:22,414
Keep latest version of each payment

48
00:03:22,864 --> 00:03:25,244
Store invalid payments separately

49
00:03:25,694 --> 00:03:27,703
Match orders with their payments

50
00:03:28,153 --> 00:03:30,591
Create financial summary report

51
00:03:33,241 --> 00:03:34,134
Part five.

52
00:03:34,134 --> 00:03:36,457
Validation and Quarantine.

53
00:03:39,107 --> 00:03:41,290
order I D must not be null

54
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:44,108
customer I D must not be null

55
00:03:44,558 --> 00:03:47,159
quantity must be greater than zero

56
00:03:47,609 --> 00:03:50,453
unit price must be greater than zero

57
00:03:50,903 --> 00:03:53,933
order status must be in approved set

58
00:03:54,383 --> 00:03:58,702
Calculate expected amount equals quantity times unit price

59
00:04:01,352 --> 00:04:05,067
We apply all validation rules in the WHERE clause

60
00:04:05,517 --> 00:04:08,954
Only records passing ALL rules make it through

61
00:04:11,604 --> 00:04:14,414
Think of validation as a quality gate

62
00:04:17,064 --> 00:04:20,477
Invalid records must be preserved, not deleted

63
00:04:20,927 --> 00:04:24,422
We quarantine them with explicit rejection reasons

64
00:04:24,872 --> 00:04:28,633
This helps us investigate and fix upstream issues!

65
00:04:31,283 --> 00:04:34,313
We capture the FIRST rejection reason found

66
00:04:34,763 --> 00:04:38,757
Alternative: capture ALL reasons in comma-separated list

67
00:04:41,407 --> 00:04:45,030
Option A, one row per order with combined reasons

68
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:49,613
Option B, multiple rows per order, one per failure

69
00:04:50,063 --> 00:04:53,023
We choose option A for simpler analysis

70
00:04:53,473 --> 00:04:56,480
Always document your choice in production code!

71
00:04:59,130 --> 00:04:59,992
Part six.

72
00:04:59,992 --> 00:05:01,812
Payment Processing.

73
00:05:04,462 --> 00:05:07,887
Payment systems often create duplicate records

74
00:05:08,337 --> 00:05:10,218
Why do duplicates happen?

75
00:05:10,668 --> 00:05:14,789
Retries, system issues, or versioning can cause them

76
00:05:17,439 --> 00:05:21,538
Payment PAY-501 appears twice in our data!

77
00:05:24,188 --> 00:05:27,439
Keep the latest version by updated at timestamp

78
00:05:27,889 --> 00:05:30,872
Use payment record I D as tie breaker

79
00:05:31,322 --> 00:05:33,911
This ensures deterministic results

80
00:05:34,361 --> 00:05:36,927
Row number function makes this easy!

81
00:05:39,577 --> 00:05:42,607
ROW_NUMBER partitions by payment_id

82
00:05:43,057 --> 00:05:46,622
We order by updated_at DESC to get latest first

83
00:05:47,072 --> 00:05:50,462
Then payment_record_id DESC breaks ties

84
00:05:50,912 --> 00:05:54,871
Filter rn = 1 keeps only the latest version

85
00:05:57,521 --> 00:06:01,387
Deduplication keeps one record per payment ID

86
00:06:04,037 --> 00:06:06,475
payment amount must be positive

87
00:06:06,925 --> 00:06:09,328
payment status must be valid

88
00:06:09,778 --> 00:06:12,797
order I D must exist in orders table

89
00:06:13,247 --> 00:06:15,395
Detect orphan payments early!

90
00:06:18,045 --> 00:06:21,226
The subquery checks if order_id exists

91
00:06:21,676 --> 00:06:25,890
This catches orphan payments like PAY-504

92
00:06:28,540 --> 00:06:35,738
Orphan Payment, payment 504 references order 9999 which doesn't exist

93
00:06:36,188 --> 00:06:38,893
Never convert null amounts to zero!

94
00:06:39,343 --> 00:06:44,765
Case sensitivity matters, lowercase success is not equal to uppercase success

95
00:06:47,415 --> 00:06:48,486
Part seven.

96
00:06:48,486 --> 00:06:49,946
Reconciliation.

97
00:06:52,596 --> 00:06:55,627
Now we match completed orders with their payments

98
00:06:56,077 --> 00:06:59,060
We use left join to find missing payments

99
00:06:59,510 --> 00:07:01,101
Why not inner join?

100
00:07:03,751 --> 00:07:09,637
Inner join only returns matched orders, hides missing payments, and loses critical data!

101
00:07:10,087 --> 00:07:16,937
Left join returns all completed orders, shows missing payments as null, and reveals data quality

102
00:07:16,937 --> 00:07:17,436
issues!

103
00:07:20,086 --> 00:07:23,709
NULL payment means order has no successful payment

104
00:07:24,159 --> 00:07:27,247
Multiple payments might indicate data issues

105
00:07:27,697 --> 00:07:29,996
Exact match is ideal case

106
00:07:30,446 --> 00:07:33,534
COALESCE handles NULL payments gracefully

107
00:07:33,984 --> 00:07:37,467
LEFT JOIN from orders reveals missing payments

108
00:07:40,117 --> 00:07:43,728
Reconciliation produces four possible statuses

109
00:07:46,378 --> 00:07:52,566
Order 2001 is perfect, 2002 is short, 2004 is missing!

110
00:07:55,216 --> 00:07:56,187
Part eight.

111
00:07:56,187 --> 00:07:58,571
Summary and Best Practices.

112
00:08:01,221 --> 00:08:04,785
Finance needs a daily summary to close the books

113
00:08:05,235 --> 00:08:08,405
We aggregate all reconciliation results

114
00:08:08,855 --> 00:08:11,479
This is what management sees every day!

115
00:08:14,129 --> 00:08:17,089
Conditional aggregation counts each status

116
00:08:17,539 --> 00:08:20,825
Unreconciled amount shows total discrepancy

117
00:08:21,275 --> 00:08:23,957
Group by date for daily summaries

118
00:08:26,607 --> 00:08:29,567
This is the trustworthy report finance needs!

119
00:08:32,217 --> 00:08:35,155
Never silently discard bad records

120
00:08:35,605 --> 00:08:38,670
Always quarantine with explicit reasons

121
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:41,755
Deduplicate before reconciliation

122
00:08:42,205 --> 00:08:45,119
Use left join to reveal missing data

123
00:08:45,569 --> 00:08:48,181
Handle nulls explicitly with coalesce

124
00:08:48,631 --> 00:08:51,638
Create audit trails for investigations

125
00:08:54,288 --> 00:09:01,458
Anti patterns include converting null to zero, using inner join only, deleting bad records, and

126
00:09:01,458 --> 00:09:02,891
ignoring duplicates

127
00:09:03,341 --> 00:09:10,221
Best practices include quarantining invalid data, using left join, preserving records with reasons,

128
00:09:10,221 --> 00:09:12,653
and deduplicating deterministically

129
00:09:15,303 --> 00:09:16,543
Practice 1 of 1.

130
00:09:16,543 --> 00:09:20,806
What happens if we use INNER JOIN instead of LEFT JOIN?

131
00:09:20,806 --> 00:09:23,673
Pause the video and write your query.

132
00:09:24,123 --> 00:09:24,123


133
00:09:26,773 --> 00:09:30,268
Inner join only shows orders that HAVE payments

134
00:09:30,718 --> 00:09:33,957
Missing payments become invisible to the business

135
00:09:34,407 --> 00:09:37,646
This is a dangerous data quality blind spot!

136
00:09:40,296 --> 00:09:43,466
Memory hook: LEFT reveals what's missing

137
00:09:46,116 --> 00:09:46,994
Quick quiz!

138
00:09:46,994 --> 00:09:48,032
Question one.

139
00:09:48,032 --> 00:09:51,944
What SQL pattern helps you find missing payments?

140
00:09:51,944 --> 00:09:53,221
Pause and think.

141
00:09:53,671 --> 00:09:53,671


142
00:09:54,121 --> 00:09:58,079
Use LEFT JOIN starting from orders to reveal NULL payments!

143
00:09:58,079 --> 00:10:01,969
Never use INNER JOIN when you need to detect missing data.

144
00:10:04,619 --> 00:10:08,799
Split payments, one order with multiple successful payments

145
00:10:09,249 --> 00:10:12,848
Refunds represented as negative payment amounts

146
00:10:13,298 --> 00:10:16,235
Late payments that arrive the next day

147
00:10:16,685 --> 00:10:20,319
Status changes where a payment later becomes refunded

148
00:10:20,769 --> 00:10:24,322
Penny differences due to rounding in different systems

149
00:10:24,772 --> 00:10:29,439
Case sensitivity issues with lowercase success versus uppercase

150
00:10:32,089 --> 00:10:34,144
Customer places one order

151
00:10:34,594 --> 00:10:36,243
First partial payment

152
00:10:36,693 --> 00:10:38,423
Second partial payment

153
00:10:38,873 --> 00:10:41,682
Sum of payments equals order amount

154
00:10:42,132 --> 00:10:45,685
Our solution already handles this with sum function!

155
00:10:48,335 --> 00:10:52,004
Modern systems use automated reconciliation daily

156
00:10:54,654 --> 00:10:59,309
These concepts come up frequently in data engineering interviews

157
00:10:59,759 --> 00:11:01,501
What should I focus on?

158
00:11:01,951 --> 00:11:05,306
Understand the why behind each design decision

159
00:11:07,956 --> 00:11:11,253
Question, why quarantine instead of delete?

160
00:11:11,703 --> 00:11:15,802
Answer, enables root cause analysis and system fixes

161
00:11:16,252 --> 00:11:19,816
Question, why deduplicate before reconciliation?

162
00:11:20,266 --> 00:11:24,155
Answer, prevents double counting and inflated totals

163
00:11:24,605 --> 00:11:27,879
Question, how does inner join hide problems?

164
00:11:28,329 --> 00:11:32,219
Answer, it drops unmatched orders, losing visibility

165
00:11:34,869 --> 00:11:39,234
C T Es break complex queries into logical steps

166
00:11:39,684 --> 00:11:43,422
Window functions enable deduplication with row number

167
00:11:43,872 --> 00:11:47,007
Left join reveals missing relationships

168
00:11:47,457 --> 00:11:50,696
Coalesce handles null values explicitly

169
00:11:51,146 --> 00:11:55,024
Conditional aggregation counts by status in one query

170
00:11:57,674 --> 00:11:58,940
Let's recap.

171
00:11:59,390 --> 00:12:03,349
You learned data quality validation and quarantine patterns

172
00:12:03,799 --> 00:12:07,235
You mastered payment deduplication with row number

173
00:12:07,685 --> 00:12:11,458
You built order payment reconciliation with left join

174
00:12:11,908 --> 00:12:15,206
You created a trustworthy daily financial summary

175
00:12:15,656 --> 00:12:19,011
Tomorrow we'll tackle slowly changing dimensions!

176
00:12:19,461 --> 00:12:23,516
Next time: Day 3: Slowly Changing Dimensions.

177
00:12:23,516 --> 00:12:24,778
See you there!

